WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:13.315
A class histogram after 20,000 requests shows what fills the heap: 43 MB of byte arrays, and 20,000 cached responses nobody meant to keep.

2
00:00:13.315 --> 00:00:15.845
It can't tell you who holds them.

3
00:00:16.533 --> 00:00:17.703
A heap dump can.

4
00:00:17.703 --> 00:00:23.332
It is a file with every object on the heap, and every reference between them.

5
00:00:23.332 --> 00:00:29.545
jcmd writes one from a running JVM, or the JVM writes one when it runs out of memory.

6
00:00:30.333 --> 00:00:32.361
Here is the graph inside it.

7
00:00:32.361 --> 00:00:34.460
A static field holds the map.

8
00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:41.266
The map holds a table, the table holds 20,000 nodes, and each node holds a key and a response.

9
00:00:41.266 --> 00:00:43.800
Each response holds a 2 KB receipt.

10
00:00:43.933 --> 00:00:51.407
A currency catalog, another GC root, holds a shared EUR string, which the responses point to as well.

11
00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:56.510
An object stays alive while a path of references reaches it from a GC root.

12
00:00:56.510 --> 00:00:58.867
The collector frees everything else.

13
00:00:59.067 --> 00:01:00.986
Every object has two sizes.

14
00:01:00.986 --> 00:01:05.323
Its shallow size is its own bytes, the header and its fields.

15
00:01:05.323 --> 00:01:07.599
The map itself is only 64 bytes.

16
00:01:07.733 --> 00:01:11.234
Its retained size is what would be freed if it went away.

17
00:01:11.234 --> 00:01:15.043
That is the object, plus everything reachable only through it.

18
00:01:15.267 --> 00:01:21.879
Cut the map loose, and the table, the nodes, the keys, the responses and every receipt become garbage.

19
00:01:21.879 --> 00:01:26.352
The shared EUR string does not, because the catalog still reaches it.

20
00:01:27.267 --> 00:01:28.296
Add it up.

21
00:01:28.296 --> 00:01:37.350
20,000 receipts at 2,064 bytes, keys at 80, responses at 32, nodes at 32, and the table.

22
00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:41.260
About 44 MB, held by a 64-byte object.

23
00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:47.176
Every path from a root to a receipt goes through the map, so the map dominates the receipts.

24
00:01:47.176 --> 00:01:51.760
EUR has a second path, from the catalog, so the map does not dominate it.

25
00:01:52.733 --> 00:01:58.928
Eclipse MAT turns the graph into a dominator tree, sorted by retained size, biggest first.

26
00:01:58.928 --> 00:02:03.264
For this dump, the map should sit at the top, with about 44 MB.

27
00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:10.148
Follow its path to a GC root, and it ends at the field that holds it: the static IDEMPOTENCY_CACHE.

28
00:02:10.933 --> 00:02:13.615
In Java and in Kotlin, it is one line.

29
00:02:13.615 --> 00:02:17.709
Every request adds an entry, and nothing ever removes one.

30
00:02:17.867 --> 00:02:19.871
The histogram said byte arrays.

31
00:02:19.871 --> 00:02:23.299
The dominator tree says this map, held by this field.
